</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Speech AI, SoftBank Intuitions, Tokyo, Japan
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Simultaneous machine translation (SiMT) has traditionally relied on offline machine translation models coupled with human-engineered heuristics or learned policies. We propose Hikari, a policy-free, fully end-to-end model that performs simultaneous speech-to-text translation and streaming transcription by encoding READ/WRITE decisions into a probabilistic WAIT token mechanism. We also introduce Decoder Time Dilation, a mechanism that reduces autoregressive overhead and ensures a balanced training distribution. Additionally, we present a supervised fine-tuning strategy that trains the model to recover from delays, significantly improving the quality-latency trade-off. Evaluated on English-to-Japanese, German, and Russian, Hikari achieves new state-of-the-art BLEU scores in both low- and high-latency regimes, outperforming recent baselines.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>streaming, machine translation ICML
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Simultaneous translation has emerged to meet the need for uninterrupted, almost real-time translation. A good simultaneous translation must not only be accurate and complete, but also not lag unacceptably far behind the original message. This requires simultaneous translators (or more commonly called interpreters) to strategically decide whether to translate what has been said so far – often making assumptions about the speaker’s intended message – or delay translation until more information is revealed by later context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ilyukhin, 2001</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chernov, 2004</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Setton, 2005</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amos et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.11578v1/Hero3.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:471/537;" width="471" height="537"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span> The challenge and reality of simultaneous translation. (Top) Conceptual illustration of syntactic divergence between English (SVO) and Japanese (SOV); the translator must ”hold” the main verb ”met” until the end of the Japanese sentence. (Bottom) Actual inference timeline, showing how Hikari-medium has learned the strategic patience required for syntactically divergent languages. Horizontal segments represent the model waiting for context (READING, i.e. emitting <span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> tokens not shown for clarity), while the steep ”steps” are rapid token emission (WRITING) once semantic ambiguity is resolved.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This decision-making is especially challenging for syntactically divergent language pairs such as English and Japanese. English provides the principal subject and verb early (SVO), whereas Japanese, a head-final (SOV) language, requires these elements – along with complex relative clauses – to be restructured. As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (top), when translating the sentence “I met the man who was wearing a red hat and carrying a blue suitcase,” the translator (human or machine) must remember/buffer the core components (the subject and the main verb “met”) for several seconds while it processes the long English descriptive clause. The Japanese output cannot be safely committed until the entire qualifying phrase (“red hat wearing, blue suitcase carrying”) has been heard, resulting in an inherent temporal lag.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The machine translation research community has attempted to model this decision-making process either with learned READ/WRITE<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          where “READ” and “WRITE” refer to ingesting more input audio and committing partial translation, respectively.</span></span></span> policies or human-crafted heuristics, designed to break input audio (or text in case of a cascaded system) along some sensible semantic or syntactic boundaries, and feed each chunk into another, usually pre-trained, machine translation (MT) model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In a departure from those approaches, our proposed model, which we call <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hikari</em>, follows a policy-free approach, where READ and WRITE actions are encoded in the probability distribution of the next token. If the model is uncertain, the probability of a text token drops, and the probability of a special <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> token rises. This unifies the “what” (translation) and the “when” (policy) into a single probabilistic framework. Thus, Hikari jointly learns an audio-conditioned language model and an implicit READ/WRITE policy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a unified speech-to-text framework that jointly learns translation and transcription without external READ/WRITE policies.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce Decoder Time Dilation, a mechanism that addresses the dominance of <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> tokens in training data by increasing the decoder’s effective step size relative to the audio.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop a fine-tuning method that trains the model to actively minimize lag and recover from latency drift during inference.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our model establishes new SOTA in English-to-Japanese, German, and Russian simultaneous translation of long-form speech, outperforming current baselines in both low- and high-latency scenarios.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide open access to our model weights and codebase to foster reproducibility and research in streaming sequence modeling.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Cascaded Systems and Heuristic Policies</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditionally, simultaneous speech-to-text translation (SiMT) has relied on cascaded architectures where an offline<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Offline MT models translate after processing the full input, rather than incrementally.</span></span></span> machine translation (MT) model (e.g. mBART <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2020b</a>)</cite>, originally developed for sentence-level translation) is paired with human-engineered heuristics. Early efforts frequently utilized fixed policies such as “wait-k” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2019</a>)</cite> or “wait-if” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cho and Esipova, 2016</a>)</cite>. However, these policies struggle, especially with syntactically divergent language pairs. If a critical verb appears at the end of a German sentence (SOV structure in subordinate clauses), a <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wait-k</span> model with a small <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is forced to hallucinate the verb to satisfy the policy, resulting in catastrophic quality loss. Conversely, a large <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> incurs unacceptable latency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Adaptive and Learned Policies</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address the rigidity of heuristics, subsequent research introduced learned policies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alinejad et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Satija and Pineau, 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grissom II et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, attention-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2020b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Papi et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Papi et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arivazhagan et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raffel et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiu and Raffel, 2018</a>)</cite>, binary search strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and approaches leveraging the stability of output predictions through “local agreement” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Polák et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ko et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2020a</a>)</cite>. While more adaptive, these methods either introduce architectural complexities or involve training a “controller” that is disconnected from the rich semantic world of the translator.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.11578v1/Figure1b.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/174;" width="405" height="174" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span> High-level overview of the model architecture.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Foundation Models and SiMT</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advancements have increasingly utilized large-scale foundation models, such as Qwen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Gemma 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2025</a>)</cite>, Llama 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and NLLB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2024</a>)</cite> in both cascaded and end-to-end frameworks. Systems like BeaverTalk <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raffel et al., 2025</a>)</cite> employ conversational prompting with memory banks, while InfiniSST <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> and the NAIST 2025 system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al., 2025</a>)</cite> use chunk-wise causal encoders or external segmentation tactics like SHAS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tsiamas et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Despite their power, these models often still depend on modular components (e.g. a separate VAD model as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Macháček and Polák (2025)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shan et al. (2024)</a></cite>) to manage the decision of when to emit text, which can complicate inference on unbounded speech streams.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Policy-Free Paradigm</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In a notable departure from these modular designs, later works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koshkin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> attempted to encapsulate the READ/WRITE supervision directly into the training data made through “causal alignment”. Their cascaded SiMT system, an LLM was fine-tuned to be both the translator and READ/WRITE policy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Hikari follows this design philosophy by building on the “causal alignment” technique and extending it to the speech-to-text domain to build a policy-free, fully end-to-end SiMT architecture. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the model strategically pauses for nearly two seconds while ingesting a complex English relative clause, only to “burst” the corresponding Japanese translation the moment the head noun (“hat’) provides semantic closure. Hikari also introduces <em id="S2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Decoder Time Dilation</em> to solve the overrepresentation of filler tokens – a known problem in alignment-based streaming systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labiausse et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Architecture</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our architecture (Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.2 Adaptive and Learned Policies ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is based on the Whisper encoder-decoder Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al. (2023)</a></cite>. While the original Whisper model is designed for <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">offline</em> processing – requiring the entire 30 s audio window to be encoded <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">before</em> generation – we introduce three critical modifications to enable native, policy-free SiMT. First, we encapsulate the READ/WRITE decision-making process into the model’s vocabulary by introducing a special <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> token. In this framework, the emission of <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> represents a READ action, while the emission of any text token represents a WRITE action. The decoder therefore generates a sequence of READ/WRITE actions that are <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">temporally synchronized</em> with the encoder. Second, to support incremental audio ingestion, we apply a standard lower-triangular causal mask to all encoder self-attention blocks. Third, to enforce strict temporal monotonicity and prevent the decoder from attending to the encoder’s future keys during training, we apply a dilated causal mask to the cross-attention.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decoder time dilation.</span> As in the original Whisper model, the input waveform (mono, sampled at 16000 Hz, with a maximum duration of 30 s) is transformed into a log-mel spectrogram with 80-dimensional feature vectors, each capturing 10 ms of input audio. The spectrogram is temporally downsampled by a factor of 2 by the convolutional layers to 1500 audio embeddings which are fed into the encoder.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.11578v1/density.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/314;" width="471" height="314"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span> Choosing the optimal value of decoder time dilation (<math id="S3.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>). Left panel: percentage of wait tokens in a 30 s en-ja sample as a function of <math id="S3.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\{1,4,6,10\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\{1,4,6,10\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Lower <math id="S3.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> improves precision of causal alignment, but also (undesirably) increases the dominance of <span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> tokens in the training data. Right panel: with too high a value of <math id="S3.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. 6 and 10), target tokens corresponding to 30 s of input audio might not fully fit into the decoder’s context (shown by dashed vertical lines for <math id="S3.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\{1,4,6,10\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\{1,4,6,10\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.11578v1/RTF_extended_2.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/270;" width="405" height="270"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Real-time factor (RTF) as a function of decoder time dilation (<math id="S3.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>) on a single A100 or H100 GPU. The plot compares Hikari-medium across varying batch sizes (<math id="S3.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\{1,10,20\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\{1,10,20\}</annotation></semantics></math>). The dashed blue line indicates the real-time threshold (RTF <math id="S3.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>); values below this line indicate generation speed faster than the input audio stream.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While the original Whisper model generates output tokens autoregressively based on the <em id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">entire</em> 30 s window, our model should generate a new token for each new <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> audio embeddings. We could simply set <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=1</annotation></semantics></math>, but in that case about 90% (Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Architecture ‣ 3 Method ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) of the tokens that the decoder is expected to generate would be <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> tokens<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Since <span id="footnote3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> doesn’t exist in Whisper’s tokenizer, we use token 93, corresponding to the tilde character (“<math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>”) that never occurs in our training set, as <span id="footnote3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span>.</span></span></span>, often appearing in long sequences uninterrupted by non-wait tokens. We found such an imbalance (with <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>) to cause the model to excessively generate those <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> tokens at inference. One way to address this is either to penalize the loss on the <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> token at training or reduce the log-likelihood of the corresponding token at inference. However, a better strategy is to “dilate” decoder time by choosing a greater value of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. This reduces the output token rate, the frequency of <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span>, as well as the number of forward passes through the decoder corresponding to a 30 s of input audio. Valid choices of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> range between 1 and approximately 4 (Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Architecture ‣ 3 Method ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). Setting a larger value of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> would increase the length of audio corresponding to one decoder token (e.g. with <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=10</annotation></semantics></math> one output token would correspond to 200 ms of speech), not only reducing the temporal precision of causal alignment, but also (and most importantly), making it impossible for all the target tokens to always fit into the decoder’s context window at high rates of speech (in WPM). With <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=4</annotation></semantics></math>, all the tokens always fully fit in the decoder’s context (Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Architecture ‣ 3 Method ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), ensuring a good tradeoff between, on the one hand, the granularity of causal alignment, and proportion of <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> tokens in the training set and the whole model’s real-time factor (RTF) (Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.1 Architecture ‣ 3 Method ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), on the other. Furthermore, we analyze the model’s throughput under batched conditions (Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.1 Architecture ‣ 3 Method ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), which is critical for scalable deployment. Hikari-medium requires <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> to support batch sizes up to 10 on a single H100 GPU in real time. At <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D&lt;4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D&lt;4</annotation></semantics></math>, the computational cost of the autoregressive decoder makes high-concurrency serving impractical (RTF <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;1.0</annotation></semantics></math>), and therefore, we report our results obtained with <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=4</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the cross-attention mask is as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{m,n}=\begin{cases}0&amp;if $n\leq 4(m-1)$\\
-\infty&amp;otherwise\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{m,n}=\begin{cases}0&amp;if $n\leq 4(m-1)$\\
-\infty&amp;otherwise\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where the decoder’s queries and encoder’s keys are indexed by <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{1,\dots,375\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>375</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{1,\dots,375\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{1,\dots,1500\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1500</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{1,\dots,1500\}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our use of a fixed decoder time dilation factor follows a somewhat similar philosophy to fixed-ratio multimodal synchronization seen in recent unified models like MinMo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Data</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ideally, Hikari would be trained on authentic audio recordings paired with professional simultaneous interpretations. However, such corpora are rare and mostly limited to bureaucratic and diplomatic discourse in official UN languages<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Arabic, Chinese, English, French, Russian, and Spanish</span></span></span>, which might not generalize to spontaneous speech or other domains. Instead, we developed a pipeline to generate synthetic training data, establishing temporal correspondences between source audio and target translations through the causal alignment process detailed below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Preparation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Briefly, the data preparation pipeline involves the following three steps. First, we use Faster Whisper<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              https://github.com/SYSTRAN/faster-whisper</span></span></span> to obtain transcripts with word-level timestamps of the input audio, such that each word and its immediately following punctuation character (if any) has its own start and end timestamp. Second, we split the transcript into sentences, and translate them one by one with an LLM. To ensure the translation consistency, the LLM is prompted to take into account the context of 4 previous sentences (see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Translation prompt ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for the full prompt template). Third, for each source and its corresponding target sentence, we perform the causal alignment procedure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koshkin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> which consists in establishing correspondences between the source and target words<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              For Japanese targets we use the <span id="footnote6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">janome</span> package to split the translation into word-like character strings.</span></span></span> based on the similarity of source and target word embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jalili Sabet et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Causal alignment</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let the transcript of one source sentence be represented as a sequence of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> words <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, where each word <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with a time interval <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[s_{i},e_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[s_{i},e_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> obtained via word-level timestamps. Here, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the start and end times of the word in milliseconds, respectively. Using a word embedding-based alignment model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jalili Sabet et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, we establish a correspondence mapping <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\{1,\dots,N\}\to\{1,\dots,M\}\cup\{\emptyset\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\{1,\dots,N\}\to\{1,\dots,M\}\cup\{\emptyset\}</annotation></semantics></math> between the target words <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=(y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=(y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> and the source words <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. For any target word <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(j)=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(j)=i</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the translation of source word <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. To ensure the model is truly simultaneous, we define a target release time <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t(y_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t(y_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> for each target word. The source and target words are considered causally aligned if:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t(y_{j})\geq e_{f(j)}+\delta" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t(y_{j})\geq e_{f(j)}+\delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{f(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{f(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the end timestamp of the corresponding source word <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is a stochastic delay <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\sim\mathcal{U}(0,200)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0,200</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\sim\mathcal{U}(0,200)</annotation></semantics></math> ms, sampled independently for each word to help reduce overfitting. The target token sequence <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed by interleaving the original tokenized target words <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with a special <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> token. The number of <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> tokens inserted before <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the minimum required such that the “logical time” of each output token matches the physical arrival of the audio information. Target words lacking a direct alignment (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(j)=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(j)=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>) are temporally shifted to occur immediately after the last aligned token. This deferral preserves original word order while ensuring causality is never violated. The formal logic for this synchronization is detailed in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Causal Alignment and WAIT Token Interleaving ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (see Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Causal Alignment and WAIT Token Interleaving ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). Note that for streaming ASR no causal alignment and translation are needed, and the target tokens represent the tokenized source transcript with <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> tokens inserted according to the corresponding words’ timestamps.</p>
</div>
