<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3 </span>Sources</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our training set comprises 62.3K hours of public and in-house data (see Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2.2 Causal alignment ‣ 3.2 Data ‣ 3 Method ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for details). While our primary focus was streaming translation of long-form audio, we deliberately included CommonVoice 2 (CV2) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ardila et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and Multilingual LibriSpeech (MLS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pratap et al., 2020</a>)</cite> – short-form datasets that offer diverse accents, speaking styles, and noise conditions, enhancing the model’s robustness. Each audio is translated into German, Japanese and Russian. Thus, we train the model for 4 tasks simultaneously – 3 translation tasks (en-de, en-ja and en-ru) and 1 transcription (ASR) – which are sampled uniformly during training. At training, if the sampled audio is longer than 30 s (as is the case for podcasts), instead of loading the entire audio, the dataloader returns a random section of it with the tokenized target text corresponding to that chunk. If the whole audio is shorter than 30 s, it is padded on the right with <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> to fully fill the decoder’s context window. If the sampled section of audio contains less than 4 words (e.g. music, silence or noise), the dataloader samples another until there are 4 or more words in the audio. The tokenized text is prepended with 4 special tokens: <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;|startoftranscript|&gt;</span>, taget language marker (either <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;|en|&gt;</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;|ja|&gt;</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;|ru|&gt;</span> or <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;|de|&gt;</span>), task marker (either <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;|transcribe|&gt;</span> or <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;|translate|&gt;</span> and finally the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;|notimestamps|&gt;</span> special token. Thus, assuming <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=4</annotation></semantics></math>, to generate the first token, the decoder attends to the past 12 audio embeddings (240 ms of audio, see the cross-attention mask in Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 Quality-latency tradeoff ‣ 4 Results ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
