</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Training</h3>

<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>Pre-training</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We initialize both the encoder and decoder from <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">whisper-medium<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">7</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote7.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">https://huggingface.co/openai/whisper-medium</span></span></span></span></span>. Although not strictly necessary, our experiments show that such initialization greatly accelerates convergence even though the encoder’s self-attention and the cross-attention become causal. We pre-train the model with AdamW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loshchilov and Hutter, 2019</a>)</cite> on 96 H100 GPUs with a per-device batch size of 128 (which approximately corresponds to 102 h of audio) for a total of 35K steps, out of which 100 steps are a linear warmup to a peak learning rate of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.00004" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.00004</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.00004</annotation></semantics></math> followed by a linear decay to zero. Also we use gradient accumulation over 2 steps and set the gradient clipping hyperparameter to 1.0.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2 </span>Supervised fine-tuning</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the model is functional immediately after pre-training, it occasionally exhibits a tendency to over-generate consecutive <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> tokens. These unnatural pauses not only increase translation latency but can also push the decoder state out of distribution, leading to generation failures. To mitigate this, we fine-tune the model on specially constructed samples that simulate recovery from high-latency scenarios. In those samples, the delay to be recovered from is introduced via a two-step process. First, a pivot index <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{start}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{start}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(4,N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(4,N)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is decoder’s context length. Second, letting <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{right}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{right}</annotation></semantics></math> be the count of <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> tokens in the segment <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y[s_{start}:]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y[s_{start}:]</annotation></semantics></math>, a displacement <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta s\sim\mathcal{U}(1,W_{right}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta s\sim\mathcal{U}(1,W_{right}-1)</annotation></semantics></math> is selected. Content tokens following the pivot are deferred by <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta s</annotation></semantics></math> positions, effectively accumulating <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> tokens to force the model to learn rapid ”burst” recovery. Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3.3.2 Supervised fine-tuning ‣ 3.3 Training ‣ 3 Method ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> illustrates this process, showing how tokens A, B, C, and D are delayed in a sample sequence. Crucially, we mask the loss for the artificially inserted <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> tokens and all preceding history. This ensures the model does not learn to imitate this delayed behavior but is instead optimized to “catch up”. This training phase uses a mixture of original, i.e. same as during pre-training, (70%) and SFT (30%) samples to maintain general translation capability while reducing latency.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.11578v1/Figure3a.svg" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/154;" width="452" height="154"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span> Construction of an SFT sample. The shaded transparent box indicates the chosen tokens to be delayed. The red rectangles illustrate the tokens for which cross-entropy is calculated. Orange and green squares are non-wait and <span id="S3.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> tokens, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">After the pre-training stage, we fine-tune the model for 2000 steps with the same per-device batch size and warm-up schedule as in pre-training, followed by a constant learning rate of 0.00004.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Inference</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At inference we use a fixed-size sliding window. For the audio encoder, we process input in 80 ms (<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\text{ ms}\times D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> ms</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\text{ ms}\times D</annotation></semantics></math>) increments. At each step, the new audio chunk is appended to the buffer; if the total duration is less than 30 s, the input is padded to 30 s. Once the buffer exceeds 30 s, we maintain a rolling window by discarding the oldest audio chunk before appending the new one, ensuring the encoder always receives exactly 30 s of context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A similar sliding window is applied to the decoder. We use greedy decoding. Generation begins with 4 prompt tokens. As new tokens are generated, they are appended to the context until the sequence reaches a maximum length of 375 tokens. Beyond this limit, for every new token generated, the earliest non-prompt token (i.e., the fifth token in the sequence) is removed. This preserves the initial prompt while keeping the decoder’s context window bounded.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Results</h2>

<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Consolidated translation performance across IWSLT25Instruct and HQ Podcasts datasets. We report BLEU and StreamLAAL (computationally aware values in parentheses) for the best Hikari-medium checkpoint (Pre-trained + SFT). Baselines for en-ja and en-de are drawn from the IWSLT 2025 evaluation campaign report. Note: Seamless results are provided for completeness but are italicized where the model exceeded the strict latency upper bound of the High Latency (HL) regime. “–” indicates no submission; “N/A” indicates the metric was not reported.<span id="S4.T3.4" class="ltx_note ltx_centering ltx_role_nicematrix-placeholder"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">nicematrix-placeholder: </span>NiceTabular* (nicematrix)</span></span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Translation quality and latency</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our model in both low- (LL) and high-latency (HL) regimes against the most recent baselines from the IWSLT 2025 speech-to-text simultaneous track, and we use the IWSLT25Instruct<span id="footnote8" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">8</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            https://iwslt.org/2025/simultaneous</span></span></span> as the test set. The results for CUNI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Macháček and Polák, 2025</a>)</cite>, CMU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, NAIST <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, OSU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raffel et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and UPV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iranzo-Sánchez et al., 2025</a>)</cite> presented in Tables <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4 Results ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> are taken directly from the campaign’s summary report <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdulmumin et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. The definitions of LL and HL for en-ja and en-de are the same as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdulmumin et al. (2025)</a></cite>, specifically: LL refers to 0–2 s for en-de (0–3.5 s for en-ja) and HL is 2–4s for en-de (3.5–5 s for en-ja). For en-ru, LL and HL are defined here as 0–2 and 2–5 s, respectively. We additionally evaluate our model’s performance on held-out subsets of HQ Podcasts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Translation quality is reported in units of BLEU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Papineni et al., 2002</a>)</cite>, and for latency we chose streaming length-adaptive average lagging (StreamLAAL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Papi et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and its computationally-aware (CA) version which not only takes into account how much the target lags behind the source, but also the hardware delay. Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2.3 Sources ‣ 3.2 Data ‣ 3 Method ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides a summary of evaluation data. All performance metrics were obtained using the SimulEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2020a</a>)</cite> package<span id="footnote9" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">9</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            https://github.com/facebookresearch/SimulEval</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Quality-latency tradeoff</h3>

<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.11578v1/Figure2.svg" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:452/377;" width="452" height="377"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span> Quality-latency tradeoff. Note how SFT shifts the Pareto frontier towards smaller latencies. Expected latencies are obtained by forcing the model to output the reference (“oracle”) tokens.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In simultaneous translation, there is an intrinsic tradeoff between quality and latency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bangalore et al., 2012</a>)</cite>: delaying output allows the model to capture more context and improve accuracy, while emitting tokens early reduces latency at the cost of potential errors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although Hikari does not rely on explicit policies (e.g., wait-k) to control this behavior, we can probe its latency response through inference-time logit biasing. By applying varying penalties to the <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> token, we can modulate the model’s threshold for emitting text, forcing it to generate translation earlier or allowing it to buffer more audio. As evidenced by the shift in the quality-latency curves (Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 Quality-latency tradeoff ‣ 4 Results ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), the SFT model achieves an overall better trade-off profile, maintaining comparable BLEU scores with latencies generally lower than the base model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Streaming ASR performance (WER %). Results for whisper-medium.en, whisper-large-v3, Parakeet and DSM-ASR are reproduced from Table 2 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeghidour et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
