<td id="S4.T4.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T4.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:50%;">2.9</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T4.2.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:50%;">12.3</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T4.2.5.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:50%;">5.7</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T4.2.5.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:50%;">10.6</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T4.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:50%;">Ours</span></th>
<td id="S4.T4.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T4.2.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:50%;">8.6</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T4.2.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:50%;">19.9</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T4.2.6.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:50%;">16.2</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T4.2.6.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:50%;">54.1</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Streaming ASR performance</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the primary design objective of Hikari is simultaneous translation, we evaluate its capability as a streaming ASR system to get a sense of what this fully causal architecture is capable of after training on a relatively modest amount of data. Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.2 Quality-latency tradeoff ‣ 4 Results ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> compares our model against widely adopted streaming baselines, including Whisper-large-v3, Parakeet-0.6B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and the recent DSM-ASR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeghidour et al., 2025</a>)</cite> on 4 benchmarks: TED-LIUM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hernandez et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, Meanwhile <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, Earnings21 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Del Rio et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and Rev16 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is important to contextualize these results within the training constraints. State-of-the-art ASR systems are typically trained on massive datasets (e.g., 680k hours for Whisper, and over 1M hours for DSM-ASR); in contrast, Hikari was trained on approximately 62k hours – more than an order of magnitude less data – with ASR treated only as an auxiliary task. Despite this data disparity, Hikari still demonstrates competence on clean low-noise audio with a WER of 8.6% on TED-LIUM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A performance gap remains on domain-specific and noisy datasets such as Earnings21 (54.1%). We attribute this primarily to the domain mismatch between our training distribution (predominantly podcasts and audiobooks, see Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2.2 Causal alignment ‣ 3.2 Data ‣ 3 Method ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and the specialized terminology found in these evaluation sets. Unlike the baselines, which likely observed significant amounts of similar data during pre-training, our model operates in a near zero-shot capacity for these domains. The stark contrast between performance on clean and challenging out-of-distribution data suggests that the architecture itself is sound, and the remaining gap is a data scaling issue rather than a fundamental limitation of the policy-free approach.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Effect of SFT on translation quality and latency. StreamLAAL CA is shown in brackets. Best BLEU scores are shown for StreamLAAL under 4 s. Average difference represents the mean change in BLEU and StreamLAAL across evaluated latency regimes.</figcaption>
