<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Ablations and other experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Effect of SFT</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that the base model exhibits systematic latency drift, consistently exceeding the theoretical latency floor (indicated by dashed vertical lines in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 Quality-latency tradeoff ‣ 4 Results ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). This behavior typically arises when the model generates extended sequences of <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> tokens during periods of silence or ambiguity. These unnaturally long pauses push the decoder into an out-of-distribution state, frequently causing it to become “stuck” in a generation loop or fail to resynchronize with the source audio once the input resumes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While penalizing the <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> token logits at inference can force lower latency, this naive approach often degrades translation quality by truncating necessary context. In contrast, our SFT strategy (Section <a href="#S3.SS3.SSS2" title="3.3.2 Supervised fine-tuning ‣ 3.3 Training ‣ 3 Method ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3.2</span></a>) explicitly trains the model to recover from induced delays. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 Quality-latency tradeoff ‣ 4 Results ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, SFT effectively shifts the quality-latency Pareto frontier to the left (compare thick vs. thin curves), enabling the model to “catch up” to the source with little or no loss of quality. Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.3 Streaming ASR performance ‣ 4 Results ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> quantifies this impact, showing that SFT yields an approximate 1-second reduction in StreamLAAL across most tasks while maintaining BLEU parity with the base model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides a real example of how Hikari efficiently recovers from delays. Note how the final burst (from 9.2s onwards) completes a sequence of 10+ Japanese tokens in under a second. This demonstrates that the SFT “catch-up” training enables the model to reduce accumulated lag without sacrificing the syntactic integrity of the target language.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Effect of model size</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the impact of parameter scale, we trained Hikari-small, initialized from Whisper-small. As summarized in Table <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ C.1 Scaling Performance with Hikari-small ‣ Appendix C Extended Ablation Studies: Model Size and Encoder Variants ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (see Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Extended Ablation Studies: Model Size and Encoder Variants ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>), the smaller variant proved significantly less robust than the medium model, suffering from both lower peak BLEU scores and poor latency control. This suggests that the ”policy-free” paradigm benefits from larger model capacity to simultaneously manage translation fidelity and causal alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, we experimented with a variant using a frozen Mimi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Défossez et al., 2024</a>)</cite> speech encoder (<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>39M parameters). Preliminary results indicate a notable performance gap compared to our medium-sized model, likely due to the reduced capacity and frozen state of the encoder. Detailed metrics and a comparative analysis of these architectures are provided in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Extended Ablation Studies: Model Size and Encoder Variants ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Multitask learning and encoder grounding</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigated the impact of the auxiliary ASR task by training a variant of Hikari-medium exclusively on translation objectives. As shown in Table <a href="#A3.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ C.3 The Role of ASR for Translation Performance ‣ Appendix C Extended Ablation Studies: Model Size and Encoder Variants ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> (Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Extended Ablation Studies: Model Size and Encoder Variants ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>), removing ASR supervision led to a significant degradation in both BLEU scores and latency across all language pairs. These results suggest that ASR provides essential acoustic grounding for the causal encoder, preventing representation drift and facilitating the precise, low-latency alignment needed for simultaneous translation. Further details and the full ablation table are provided in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Extended Ablation Studies: Model Size and Encoder Variants ‣ Streaming Translation and Transcription Through Speech-to-Text Causal Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusions and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are a few ways in which Hikari could be improved or extended. First, our ablations indicate a significant performance gap between the small and medium models. Future research could explore scaling Hikari to larger architectures (to the scale of Whisper-large and beyond) to push the limits of translation quality. Thankfully, given its current RTF, this size increase will still allow Hikari to work faster than real time even without any performance optimization tricks. Second, Hikari currently utilizes a fixed dilation factor (<math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=4</annotation></semantics></math>). While it already provides a good balance between alignment precision and token density, a dynamic dilation mechanism that adjusts the generation rate based on real-time speech density could further improve the quality-latency tradeoff. Third, besides logit manipulation of the <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WAIT</span> token at inference, there are other, potentially better, ways to control latency – e.g. through speculative decoding, or controlling the latency regime via a special token during generation. Fourth, the current Hikari model could be used as the backbone in a streaming speech-to-speech translation model (e.g. via continued training with an RQ-transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, similarly to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeghidour et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Finally, our training set was dominated by podcasts, and the inclusion of more challenging/noisy data, covering a wider range of domains, could further boost Hikari’s streaming ASR and translation performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Impact Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper advances the field of Machine Learning by introducing a policy-free, end-to-end framework for simultaneous speech-to-text translation and transcription. By establishing an implicit READ/WRITE policy directly from causally aligned audio-text pairs, our approach eliminates the need for complex human-engineered heuristics or external decision-making modules. We anticipate this technology will significantly facilitate real-time cross-lingual communication at international conferences and in education. In support of open science, we release our inference code and model weights to the research community. At the same time, we encourage practitioners to remain mindful of risks regarding data privacy and potential algorithmic biases that may be learned from the large-scale datasets used for training.</p>
</div>
</section>
