<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Frozen Mimi Encoder</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To explore the utility of using a high-quality pre-trained streaming speech encoder, we replaced the Whisper-based causal encoder with a frozen Mimi encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Défossez et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, specifically designed for high-efficiency streaming audio compression and providing a much smaller footprint (its encoder part is only <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>39M parameters). In this variant of Hikari, we use a single trainable linear layer to project the unquantized output embeddings (of size 512) into the latent space of Hikari’s decoder (of size 1024). Note that since Mimi’s frame rate is 12.5 Hz, there’s a one to one correspondence between the number audio embeddings and Hikari’s decoder context length (375 for <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=4</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our tests, the Mimi-based variant yielded much lower translation quality and higher latency drift (far beyond the HL upper limit) than our primary model. We hypothesize that because the encoder was relatively small and frozen, the decoder had to compensate for acoustic representations that were not specifically optimized for the causal alignment task, leading to a capacity bottleneck.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T7" class="ltx_table">
